  "solution": {
    "text": "Number of moles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>in&nbsp;gm</mtext></mrow><mtext>atomic&nbsp;weight</mtext></mfrac></math><br />Number of moles&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Li</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>70</mn><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math><br />Number of moles of&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Na</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>230</mn><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math><br />Amount of energy = moles <math><mo>×</mo></math> energy needed for 1 mole.<br />So the amounts of energies required for <math><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> mole each of Li and Na are as follows<br />For sodium&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>520</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5200</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>&nbsp;<br />For Lithium&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>495</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4950</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>&nbsp;respectively."
  }
